WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.493 --> 00:00:04.029
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:06.040
- (indistinct) you name it.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.200
Welcome back.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.830
Everybody, the royal family,
the D'Amelios are here.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.380
They are known around the world

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.250
as the first family of Tik Tok.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.280
I think you're the first
family of social media.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.380
But there are some fun
facts you might not know.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.700
Did you know that Dixie
was a sick BMX biker,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.073
ranking fifth in the nation?

00:00:29.073 --> 00:00:31.200
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.240
Where does it end with you guys?

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.950
And fun fact about Charli,

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.380
she got 15 tattoos in one year.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.213
I like it.

00:00:38.213 --> 00:00:39.120
- Thank you.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.860
They're like this big.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.270
- I'm looking, I don't see any on you.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.530
- There's some on my fingers.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:44.363
- [Dixie] They're so tiny.

00:00:44.363 --> 00:00:45.780
- There's one right here.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:46.940
- [Drew] Oh, they are teeny tiny.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.888
- Oh my back.

00:00:48.888 --> 00:00:49.721
They're very small.

00:00:49.721 --> 00:00:51.360
- All right, well please welcome everybody

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.890
Charli, Dixie, Heidi

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.796
and Mark D'Amelio.

00:00:54.796 --> 00:00:57.361
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:00:57.361 --> 00:01:02.361
Now, out of curiosity, Dixie and Charli,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.180
how do you navigate having hundreds

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.830
of millions of followers,

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.640
having millions of
people know who you are?

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.550
- I feel like we don't think about it.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.870
I mean we do what we do,

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.670
and we post online,

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.230
but we just try to live our lives,

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.220
hang out with our family.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.510
We live together, we're roommates,

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.220
so we come home at the end of the day,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.510
and are sisters,

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.343
and just...

00:01:25.343 --> 00:01:28.020
- I think it's also still
having that connection

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.000
with the people that knew you before,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.580
and my hometown friends are a big part

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.016
of my life.

00:01:34.016 --> 00:01:34.890
My family's a big part of my life.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.790
And I'm still their daughter
at the end of the day,

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.680
and they have rules,

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.240
and I have to respect my parents.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.124
And she's still my big sister.

00:01:44.124 --> 00:01:46.740
It feels the same.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.398
- So boundaries,

00:01:48.398 --> 00:01:50.160
what is your advice for having

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.460
extremely recognizable
kids on social media,

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.563
and what's your advice for parents

00:01:54.563 --> 00:01:57.817
who are just going "God,
I didn't sign up for this

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:59.851
"in our generation"?

00:01:59.851 --> 00:02:03.423
- I think for us, we,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.390
the foundation is what we
set with the boundaries,

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:10.560
we're your parents, we're not,

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.150
you have friends, you don't need friends.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.430
You have parents and rules and boundaries.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.730
But from what they knew as kids,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.090
that didn't change when all
this happened on social media,

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.740
that stayed the constant.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.820
And I think them knowing
this is what it is,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.790
and it still feels the same helped.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.400
- What is your advice to parents out there

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.700
who are having kids,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.980
or even themselves trying to build

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.140
a life for themselves on social media?

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.390
Because that is a really amazing

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:46.080
new opportunity for
everybody in a positive way.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.350
- Yeah, I think the tone

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.840
has changed over time where now

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.250
everyone understands that you can

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.320
make a career on social media,

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.130
so I think it's up to
parents to get involved,

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.042
but not to be the center of attention.

00:03:01.042 --> 00:03:03.570
Heidi and I have tried to balance it out

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.690
where we're involved with the process,

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.510
but we never try to overshadow the girls.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.780
It's their show, we're
just along for the ride.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.360
- Now switching gears a little bit,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.700
I do have two daughters, sister advice.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.810
When should I lean in,

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:20.103
when should I lean out?

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.540
- Us, we hated each other.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.628
- So bad, did not talk.

00:03:25.628 --> 00:03:27.003
- [Drew] What ages?

00:03:28.013 --> 00:03:29.340
- All of middle school,

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.171
until we got into high school together,

00:03:31.171 --> 00:03:34.050
then we became best friends.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.750
She would come with me to
every party on the weekend.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.270
It just happened out of nowhere though.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.040
We would go weeks without talking.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.003
- And we lived,

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.010
we shared a wall.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.333
- Yeah.

00:03:45.333 --> 00:03:46.166
But now I don't remember the last time

00:03:46.166 --> 00:03:47.070
we actually argued.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.190
- Yeah.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:49.023
- Over anything.

00:03:49.023 --> 00:03:50.400
- And now we live together just us two,

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.870
so that's pretty fun.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.960
I notice the times where
it gets the most annoying

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.000
when we fight is when they chime in,

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.197
and they're like "What are
you guys arguing about?"

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:00.030
- "You guys are sisters."

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:00.990
- We were already over it.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.880
We were over it 10 minutes ago.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.057
- [Heidi] We cause more problems.

00:04:04.057 --> 00:04:05.799
- [Charli And Dixie] Yes.

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:07.020
- When parents get involved
in siblings' fights,

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.000
I think that makes it so much worse.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:09.833
We're done.

00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:10.666
- We get over it.

00:04:10.666 --> 00:04:12.150
- Oh my God, I'm so excited right now,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.373
you don't even understand.

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:15.910
This is such, I feel the same way.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.920
That's my gut instinct, I'm
like let them work it out.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.170
Not about everything
or when it's dangerous,

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.183
obviously we all known the line.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:26.472
But yes, oh my God.

00:04:26.472 --> 00:04:27.305
(Drew yelling)

00:04:27.305 --> 00:04:28.138
Thank you.

00:04:28.138 --> 00:04:29.144
I'm so sorry, this is such great news.

00:04:29.144 --> 00:04:30.690
- Anytime they get involved

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:31.710
it makes it so much worse,

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.000
and then we just get mad at them.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.758
Oh my God, they're so annoying.

00:04:34.758 --> 00:04:35.591
- We're like okay, get over it.

00:04:35.591 --> 00:04:36.600
- To be fair, they'll argue,

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.774
she's texting me, she's
calling me about the other one.

00:04:39.774 --> 00:04:40.650
So-

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:41.483
- [Drew] So what do you do?

00:04:41.483 --> 00:04:42.540
How do I do this?

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.373
- I step in.

00:04:43.373 --> 00:04:44.489
- No, don't get involved.

00:04:44.489 --> 00:04:45.630
- Just listen.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:46.710
- I try to tell the-

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:47.543
- Just listen.

00:04:47.543 --> 00:04:48.930
- I try to tell each other

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.820
where the other one was
trying to come from.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.712
Meanwhile, they've already made up.

00:04:52.712 --> 00:04:56.220
He and I are still like
what should we do, whatever,

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.637
and they are so moved on.

00:04:57.637 --> 00:04:59.797
I'm like "I'm not doing this anymore.

00:04:59.797 --> 00:05:01.680
"We're going over here,
you guys figure it out."

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.300
I don't get involved anymore.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.546
- [Charli] It took a minute.

00:05:05.546 --> 00:05:06.531
- This is so helpful.

00:05:06.531 --> 00:05:10.950
You guys are doing dancing
with the stars together.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:11.783
- Us two.

00:05:11.783 --> 00:05:13.620
- So how do you do that?

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.030
How does that work?

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.380
- Well, we tried the
whole smack talk thing

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:19.770
for a few days,

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.667
and then I was like "I'm done with this

00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:24.253
"because as my competitor

00:05:24.253 --> 00:05:25.447
"I can't come to you

00:05:25.447 --> 00:05:27.480
"and be like oh my God I'm so stressed."

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:28.860
So we can't do this.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.450
And then I also have to keep in mind

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:31.590
that she is my mother,

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:35.910
and I can't be too mean
about the competition thing.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:36.873
But I don't know,

00:05:38.464 --> 00:05:39.330
I feel like we've never

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.460
competed against each other in anything,

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:42.840
so it's, we're learning.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:43.830
- Yeah.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.780
But I'm her biggest cheer,

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.220
both of their biggest cheerleader,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.750
I don't think that's gonna ever change,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.850
but at the same time I'm competitive,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.252
so I wanna win too.

00:05:52.252 --> 00:05:54.903
- [Charli] We keep going
back and forth with it.

00:05:55.937 --> 00:05:58.980
- This is definitely a weird
feeling for me as a mom to,

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:00.420
it doesn't feel right.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:01.440
So I'm figuring it out.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.810
I don't know how you get through that.

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:06.180
- And I mean you are known as a dancer.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.830
You are an incredible dancer.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:09.270
I have watched all your videos.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.880
I love your incredible,

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.100
you are such an amazing dancer.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.930
But other than dancing,

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.000
if you were to have to pick a lane,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.583
an occupation, what would it be?

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.300
- Do you remember what you used to say?

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.170
- No.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:26.880
- You wanted to be a psychologist

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.440
'cause you had a lot of bad therapists.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.570
- Oh yeah, that was one thing

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.630
because I've had my fair
share of very bad therapists.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:35.679
- I thought you wanted to run a hotel.

00:06:35.679 --> 00:06:36.570
- [Heidi] Oh, I forgot about that.

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:40.170
- Oh yeah, I was going to go
to school for hospitality.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.370
I was going to go to college for that,

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:42.300
and be on the dance team.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.467
- Well maybe you could
have a therapy hotel.

00:06:44.467 --> 00:06:46.426
(indistinct) check into one of those.

00:06:46.426 --> 00:06:48.420
- That seems like a really good vacation.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:49.253
- I'm not kidding.

00:06:49.253 --> 00:06:51.182
Actually, why is this not a thing?

00:06:51.182 --> 00:06:52.015
(Charli laughing)

00:06:52.015 --> 00:06:53.610
All right, we're not
done with the D'Amelios.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:55.320
Up next, it's gonna be the final five.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:56.640
We're getting to know this family.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:57.641
We'll be right back.

00:06:57.641 --> 00:07:00.308
